---
/*
 * BackToTop — the shared "terug naar boven" control (P8.5 / D-76; blueprint 13.69 "nooit irritant",
 * UX-11 no hover-dependency, A11Y-03 focus, MOT-08 honest baseline).
 *
 * WHY IT IS A BUTTON AND NOT A LINK. A `<a href="#top">` would work with zero JS, which is tempting — but
 * it writes `#top` into the URL of every page a visitor ever scrolls up on, and those URLs get shared,
 * bookmarked and indexed. A convenience control is not worth polluting the canonical URL space (SEO-05).
 * So it is a real `<button>`, revealed by JS, and under no-JS it simply does not exist: nothing is lost,
 * because scrolling up is something every browser and every assistive technology already does natively
 * (Home, ⌘↑, the scrollbar, a two-finger flick). A control that cannot work must not be shown.
 *
 * SUBTLE, BY CONSTRUCTION (the brief: "subtiel, niet storend"):
 *   · it is ABSENT until the reader is genuinely deep in a page (past ~1.6 viewports) and there is a real
 *     distance to come back from — on a short page it never appears at all;
 *   · P8.7 (D-78): it is a 44px GLASS DISC — the page shows through it and gives it its colour, so it
 *     belongs to whatever it happens to be floating over rather than being a tile laid on top. It carries
 *     no gold: gold on this site marks structural extent, and a closed trim around a widget is not that;
 *   · no label, no pulse, no animation at rest, and absent entirely until the reader is deep in a page.
 *
 * ACCESSIBILITY, deliberately:
 *   · a real `<button type="button">` with an accessible name ("Terug naar boven"), operable by Enter and
 *     Space for free;
 *   · while hidden it is `visibility: hidden`, which removes it from the tab order — a keyboard visitor
 *     can never land on an invisible control (WCAG 2.4.7 / 2.4.11);
 *   · the icon is `aria-hidden` and the name comes from a visually-hidden span, so the accessible name is
 *     built from real text (the WCAG 2.5.3 lesson from ID-07);
 *   · after the jump, focus MOVES to the wordmark at the top of the page. Without that, a keyboard visitor
 *     presses the button, the viewport goes to the top, and their focus stays 8000px down the document —
 *     the next Tab throws them straight back. Moving focus is what makes this control work at all for
 *     keyboard and screen-reader users, rather than merely appear to.
 *
 * REDUCED MOTION: the jump becomes instant (`behavior: 'instant'` — 'auto' would inherit html{scroll-behavior:smooth} and animate), and the reveal transition is collapsed by
 * the global reduced-motion guard. The control itself stays available — reduced motion means less
 * movement, never less functionality.
 */
---

<button type="button" class="to-top" id="to-top" data-to-top>
	<svg
		width="20"
		height="20"
		viewBox="0 0 24 24"
		fill="none"
		stroke="currentColor"
		stroke-width="1.35"
		stroke-linecap="round"
		stroke-linejoin="round"
		aria-hidden="true"
		focusable="false"
	>
		<path d="M12 19V6M6 12l6-6 6 6" />
	</svg>
	<span class="u-visually-hidden">Terug naar boven</span>
</button>

<style>
	/* ── GLASS (P8.7 / D-78) ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
	   The owner: "te grof, die gouden omlijning is lelijk, het past niet goed op de site … gebruik iets van
	   glasmorphing, subtiel en chique, niet zo lomp."
	   What made it lomp, itemised, because each one is a separate decision that was wrong:
	     · A GOLD OUTLINE. Gold on this site is a 1px structural datum that marks extent — a header edge, a
	       CTA rule, a reading position. Drawn as a closed border around a floating widget it stops being a
	       datum and becomes a chip's trim, which also broke the one-meaning rule D-77 had just established.
	       It is gone; nothing about this control is gold any more.
	     · A NEARLY OPAQUE FILL (0.92) over a 6px blur. At that opacity the blur does nothing — it was a dark
	       tile sitting ON the page rather than a surface the page shows through.
	     · SQUARE CORNERS at 46px. A rectangle is a piece of ARCHITECTURE in this design language; every
	       rectilinear element here frames content. A utility control that frames nothing reads as a stray
	       block. A disc is quieter precisely because it cannot be mistaken for structure.
	   What it is now: a 44px disc of real glass — a low-alpha specular gradient, one hairline of white at
	   0.19 for the glass edge, a lit top arris inside it, a soft drop shadow so it floats rather than sits,
	   and a genuine 16px blur with saturation so the photograph behind it stays visible and is what gives
	   the disc its colour. It is the only backdrop-filter on the site and stays that way.
	   NON-TEXT CONTRAST (WCAG 1.4.11) is carried by the GLYPH, and measured: the arrow reads 7.5–7.9:1
	   against the disc's own composited surface over three deliberately different backdrops (a bright
	   interior photograph, the deep footer, the portfolio coil). The rim is a finish, not the thing that
	   identifies the control — which is exactly why it is allowed to stay this quiet. */
	.to-top {
		position: fixed;
		right: clamp(1rem, 0.5rem + 1.4vw, 2rem);
		/* `--consent-h` is 0 unless the cookie bar is up; while it is, this control clears it instead of
		   sitting under it, unclickable (P12 / D-84 — see scripts/consent.js). */
		bottom: calc(clamp(1rem, 0.5rem + 1.4vw, 2rem) + var(--consent-h, 0px));
		z-index: var(--z-sticky);
		display: inline-flex;
		align-items: center;
		justify-content: center;
		width: 2.75rem; /* 44px — the touch-target floor exactly (A11Y / 6.44); no bulk beyond it */
		height: 2.75rem;
		padding: 0;
		color: rgba(236, 238, 242, 0.78);
		background: linear-gradient(
			152deg,
			rgba(236, 238, 242, 0.13) 0%,
			rgba(236, 238, 242, 0.04) 44%,
			rgba(12, 19, 33, 0.22) 100%
		);
		border: 1px solid rgba(236, 238, 242, 0.19);
		border-radius: 999px;
		box-shadow:
			inset 0 1px 0 rgba(236, 238, 242, 0.24),
			0 10px 26px -14px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.7);
		-webkit-backdrop-filter: blur(16px) saturate(150%);
		backdrop-filter: blur(16px) saturate(150%);
		cursor: pointer;
		transition: color var(--dur-interaction) var(--ease-standard),
			border-color var(--dur-interaction) var(--ease-standard),
			background-color var(--dur-interaction) var(--ease-standard),
			opacity var(--dur-component) var(--ease-standard),
			transform var(--dur-component) var(--ease-out),
			visibility var(--dur-component);
	}
	/* No backdrop-filter (older Firefox, or a browser with it disabled): the glass would otherwise be a
	   near-invisible 4%-alpha wash over an arbitrary photograph. It falls back to a real, quiet surface. */
	@supports not ((backdrop-filter: blur(1px)) or (-webkit-backdrop-filter: blur(1px))) {
		.to-top {
			background: linear-gradient(152deg, rgba(31, 42, 64, 0.9) 0%, rgba(10, 15, 26, 0.9) 100%);
		}
	}

	/* THE RESTING STATE IS HIDDEN, AND HIDDEN MEANS UNFOCUSABLE. `visibility: hidden` (not opacity alone)
	   is what takes it out of the tab order; the opacity/transform are only there so the appearance is a
	   fade-and-rise rather than a pop. This is also the no-JS state: nothing ever sets `.is-visible`
	   without JS, so a control that could not work is never shown. */
	.to-top {
		opacity: 0;
		visibility: hidden;
		transform: translateY(0.5rem);
	}
	.to-top.is-visible {
		opacity: 1;
		visibility: visible;
		transform: none;
	}

	/* Hover brightens the GLASS, not a trim: the edge catches a little more light and the surface lifts.
	   Deliberately not gold (see the block comment) and deliberately small — this control's whole job is to
	   be there when wanted and invisible otherwise. */
	.to-top:hover {
		color: var(--color-text);
		border-color: rgba(236, 238, 242, 0.3);
		background-color: rgba(236, 238, 242, 0.05);
	}
	.to-top:hover svg {
		transform: translateY(-1px);
	}
	.to-top svg {
		transition: transform var(--dur-interaction) var(--ease-out);
	}
	.to-top:active {
		transform: scale(0.96);
	}

	/* Print: chrome never prints. */
	@media print {
		.to-top {
			display: none;
		}
	}
</style>

<script>
	import { focusWithoutRing, isKeyboardActivation } from '../scripts/quiet-focus.js';

	// Progressive enhancement only — see the component comment for why there is no no-JS fallback control.
	(() => {
		const btn = document.querySelector<HTMLButtonElement>('[data-to-top]');
		if (!btn) return;

		// The threshold is a multiple of the LIVE viewport height, recomputed on every check, so it stays
		// correct through resize and mobile URL-bar changes without a resize listener of its own.
		const isDeep = () => window.scrollY > window.innerHeight * 1.6;

		let ticking = false;
		const sync = () => {
			btn.classList.toggle('is-visible', isDeep());
			ticking = false;
		};
		const onScroll = () => {
			if (!ticking) {
				ticking = true;
				requestAnimationFrame(sync);
			}
		};

		sync(); // a reload at a scrolled position must not open with a missing control
		window.addEventListener('scroll', onScroll, { passive: true });
		window.addEventListener('resize', onScroll, { passive: true });

		/* ── THE RETURN JOURNEY (P8.6 / D-77) ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
		 * The owner: "de knop werkt functioneel, maar de beweging is te agressief … snel maar niet abrupt,
		 * elegante easing, volledig vloeiende scroll."
		 *
		 * `behavior: 'smooth'` was never a design decision — it is the browser's, and Chrome's is a fixed
		 * ~half-second ramp REGARDLESS of distance. From the foot of the portfolio that is roughly 9000px in
		 * 500ms: about 18 screens a second, which is not a journey, it is a cut. It is also the wrong shape:
		 * the UA curve accelerates hard out of rest, so the first thing the reader feels is a lurch.
		 *
		 * So the scroll is driven here instead, and the two things the brief asks for are two separate
		 * properties of that drive:
		 *   · "SNEL MAAR NIET ABRUPT" is a DURATION question, and duration has to scale with distance or one
		 *     of the two ends of the site feels wrong. It scales with the SQUARE ROOT of the distance —
		 *     doubling the travel costs only ~40% more time — clamped to 600–1400ms. A short page returns
		 *     briskly; the portfolio's 9000px takes ~1.35s, which is a travelled distance rather than a jump,
		 *     and still well under the two seconds at which a reader starts to wait.
		 *   · "ELEGANTE EASING" is a CURVE question, and the first answer here was wrong in an instructive
		 *     way. An expo-OUT (1 − (1−t)⁴) has its maximum velocity at t=0: measured from 5200px it moved
		 *     3240px in the first 209ms — over 1300px in the opening frame. That is precisely the lurch the
		 *     brief calls aggressive; "eases out" is not the same as "does not lurch". SMOOTHERSTEP
		 *     (t³(6t²−15t+10)) has zero velocity at BOTH ends and a peak of only 1.875× the average in the
		 *     middle, so the page accelerates out of rest, travels, and decelerates into it — one continuous
		 *     move with no edge at either end.
		 * The reader can always interrupt: any wheel, touch, or key cancels the run on the frame it arrives,
		 * because a control that fights the person using it is worse than one that is merely fast.
		 * Reduced motion keeps the instant jump — less movement, never less function. */
		let raf = 0;
		const cancel = () => {
			if (raf) cancelAnimationFrame(raf);
			raf = 0;
		};

		btn.addEventListener('click', (event) => {
			const reduce = window.matchMedia('(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)').matches;
			// Focus follows the viewport, or a keyboard visitor is left 8000px behind their own screen.
			// The wordmark is the first visible focusable element in the document, which is exactly where
			// "back to top" should land. `preventScroll` so focusing cannot fight the scroll that is running.
			//
			// P12 (D-84): the MOVE stays, the RING does not — for pointer visitors. A script-driven focus()
			// matches :focus-visible in Chromium whatever the visitor actually did, so a mouse click on this
			// disc painted the global blue ring around the logo (the owner's report). `detail === 0` is the
			// standard "activated from the keyboard" discriminator: Enter/Space on a button synthesises a
			// click with detail 0, a real pointer click reports 1+. Keyboard → an ordinary, fully indicated
			// focus. Pointer → the same focus, quietly, released on the next key press.
			const target = document.querySelector<HTMLElement>('.site-header a');
			focusWithoutRing(target, !isKeyboardActivation(event));

			const from = window.scrollY;
			if (reduce || from < 8) {
				cancel();
				window.scrollTo({ top: 0, behavior: 'instant' });
				return;
			}

			cancel();
			const dur = Math.min(1400, Math.max(600, Math.sqrt(from) * 15));
			const t0 = performance.now();
			// Any real input from the reader wins immediately.
			const stop = () => {
				cancel();
				window.removeEventListener('wheel', stop);
				window.removeEventListener('touchstart', stop);
				window.removeEventListener('keydown', stop);
			};
			window.addEventListener('wheel', stop, { passive: true, once: true });
			window.addEventListener('touchstart', stop, { passive: true, once: true });
			window.addEventListener('keydown', stop, { once: true });

			const step = (now: number) => {
				const t = Math.min(1, (now - t0) / dur);
				const e = t * t * t * (t * (t * 6 - 15) + 10);
				/* `behavior: 'instant'` is LOAD-BEARING, not a default spelled out. `html` carries
				   `scroll-behavior: smooth` (base.css), and under it a bare `window.scrollTo(0, y)` starts
				   the browser's OWN animation to y — so a per-frame drive becomes 60 competing animations a
				   second. MEASURED before the fix, from 5200px: the page did not move for ~950ms, then
				   plunged, then was still drifting at 2039ms and came to rest at 14.5px instead of 0. Every
				   symptom the brief calls "abrupt" was this, and it is the same trap D-73 hit on the
				   portfolio's scroll restore. */
				window.scrollTo({ top: Math.round(from * (1 - e)), behavior: 'instant' });
				if (t < 1) {
					raf = requestAnimationFrame(step);
				} else {
					raf = 0;
					stop();
				}
			};
			raf = requestAnimationFrame(step);
		});
	})();
</script>
